      <div id="tab-theory" class="tp hidden doc">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Principle</h3>
        <p>A Wheatstone bridge has four resistors in a diamond. A DC source drives the diagonal A–D; a null detector (galvanometer) sits across the other diagonal B–C. When the potentials at B and C are equal, no current flows through the detector, and the unknown resistance follows from the other three.</p>
        <h3>Bridge equations (this lab's labelling)</h3>
        <p>R₁ = A–B, R₂ = B–D, R₃ = A–C, R₄ = C–D, with the source positive at A and D at 0 V.</p>
        <div class="eq">V_B = Vs · R₂ / (R₁ + R₂)     V_C = Vs · R₄ / (R₃ + R₄)</div>
        <div class="eq">Vo = V_B − V_C = Vs (R₂R₃ − R₁R₄) / [(R₁+R₂)(R₃+R₄)]</div>
        <div class="eq">Balance: Vo = 0 ⇒ R₁R₄ = R₂R₃ ⇒ R₁/R₂ = R₃/R₄ ⇒ Rx = R₃ · R₂/R₁</div>
        <div class="eq">Ig = Vs (R₂R₃ − R₁R₄) / [Rg(R₁+R₂)(R₃+R₄) + R₁R₂(R₃+R₄) + R₃R₄(R₁+R₂)]</div>
        <p>The balance point does not depend on Vs or Rg. Those only change how large the deflection is, which is why the null method is accurate: the result depends on resistor ratios, not on meter calibration.</p>
        <h3>Sensitivity</h3>
        <p>Near balance with four equal arms R, a small change ΔR in one arm gives ΔVo ≈ Vs · (ΔR/R) / 4. Sensitivity grows with Vs, but so does self-heating (P = V²/R), which drifts the resistance. Keep arm resistances of similar size and match Rg to the bridge resistance for best current sensitivity.</p>
        <h3>Deflection bridge: strain gauges</h3>
        <p>A strain gauge changes resistance by ΔR/R = GF · ε. Used as an unbalanced bridge, the output is proportional to strain.</p>
        <div class="eq">Quarter bridge: Vo/Vs = x / [2(2 + x)] ≈ GF·ε / 4     (x = GF·ε)</div>
        <div class="eq">Half bridge (adjacent arms, opposite strain): Vo/Vs = x / 2   (exactly linear)</div>
        <div class="eq">Full bridge (all four active): Vo/Vs = x   (exactly linear)</div>
        <h3>Limitations and errors</h3>
        <ul><li>Lead and contact resistance matter for low resistances (below about 1 Ω). A Kelvin double bridge is used there.</li>
        <li>Very high resistances give tiny currents and a weak null. Use a more sensitive detector.</li>
        <li>Thermoelectric EMFs and resistor self-heating shift the null.</li>
        <li>Resolution is limited by the smallest step of the decade box and by the detector's minimum visible deflection.</li></ul>
        <h3>Applications</h3>
        <p>Strain gauges and load cells, RTD and thermistor thermometry, pressure transducers, and precision resistance comparison in calibration labs.</p>
      </div>

